 ## Background
-The original NeRF paper [1063-nerf.md] introduced a simple MLP architecture. Subsequent research rapidly expanded the architectural landscape to address specific challenges: speed (AdaNeRF, MIMO-NeRF), geometry (Ref-NeRF, Geometric Priors), generalization (Transformer-based, MultiPlaneNeRF), and generation (pi-GAN, NeRDi). The field has moved from monolithic MLPs to hybrid architectures combining grids, transformers, and specialized heads for specific physical properties like reflection or deformation.
+The original NeRF architecture [S1, unofficial][S2] established the baseline of using an MLP to map 3D coordinates and viewing directions to density and color. Subsequent research rapidly diversified the architecture to address specific challenges:
+*   **Efficiency:** Methods like [AdaNeRF](6195-adanerf.md), [MIMO-NeRF](10928-mimo-nerf.md), and [Sparse and Efficient Representations](9326-sparse-and-efficient-representations.md) focus on reducing computational load through architectural changes or sparsity.
+*   **Single-View/Generalization:** [SinNeRF](6199-sinnerf.md), [NeRDi](9112-nerdi.md), and [Transformer-based Architectures](8998-transformer-based-architectures.md) incorporate external priors (depth, diffusion, attention) to generalize from limited data.
+*   **Semantic/Instance Understanding:** [NeRF-RPN](8663-nerf-rpn.md) and [Instance-NeRF](10611-instance-nerf.md) extend the output space to include instance labels and bounding boxes.
+*   **Physical Accuracy:** [Ref-NeRF](10843-ref-nerf.md), [IRON](10251-iron.md), and [DE-NeRF](9572-generative-and-style-based-models.md) modify the rendering equation or network outputs to handle reflections, materials, and lighting explicitly.
+*   **Dynamic/Deformable Scenes:** [Deformable Radiance Fields](9673-pronerf.md) and [NeRF in the Wild](9572-generative-and-style-based-models.md) introduce time-dependent or transient components to handle motion and changing appearances.
 
